    {
      "type": "ai",
      "data": {
        "content": "The **average density** of the Earth is approximately:\n\n### **5,513 kilograms per cubic meter (kg/m\u00b3)**  \n(or **5.513 g/cm\u00b3**).\n\nThis makes Earth the densest planet in the Solar System! \ud83c\udf0d\n\n### How is this density calculated?\nDensity is the ratio of mass to volume. Using the Earth's mass (**5.972 \u00d7 10\u00b2\u2074 kg**) and its approximate volume (**1.083 \u00d7 10\u00b9\u00b2 km\u00b3** or **1.083 \u00d7 10\u00b2\u00b9 m\u00b3**), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Density} = \\frac{\\text{Mass}}{\\text{Volume}} = \\frac{5.972 \\times 10^{24} \\, \\text{kg}}{1.083 \\times 10^{21} \\, \\text{m}^3} \\approx 5,513 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3\n\\]\n\n### Why is Earth's density so high?\nEarth's high density is due to its composition. The planet has a metallic core (mostly iron and nickel) and a rocky mantle and crust, which are made of silicate minerals. The dense core contributes significantly to the overall density.",
