      "type": "ai",
      "data": {
        "content": "The density of the **Earth** compared to the **Moon** can be expressed as a ratio by dividing their respective densities.\n\n### **Density of Earth**:\n- **5,513 kg/m\u00b3**\n\n### **Density of the Moon**:\n- **3,344 kg/m\u00b3**\n\n### **Density Ratio (Earth:Moon)**:\n\\[\n\\text{Density Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Density of Earth}}{\\text{Density of Moon}} = \\frac{5,513}{3,344} \\approx 1.65\n\\]\n\n### **Conclusion**:\nThe Earth is about **1.65 times denser** than the Moon. This difference is primarily due to the Earth's metallic core, which is much larger and denser than the Moon's core. The Moon is composed mostly of lighter silicate materials, with a much smaller iron core compared to Earth.",
